Erebo is the twelfth and outermost planet of the [[Aurelius system]], orbiting in the profound cold of the far system beyond the cobalt giant [[Centinela]]. It is a small, frozen world, classified as a dwarf planet or a major Kuiper-type outlier. Unlike the gas and ice giants that precede it, Erebo is a solid, rocky body, serving as a silent boundary stone between the star's influence and the interstellar void.

The planet is a dark, charcoal-grey sphere heavily saturated with impact craters from billions of years of exposure to outer system debris. Its most notable visual feature is the presence of bright, irregular patches of white nitrogen ice that cling to its deepest basins and polar regions, providing a stark contrast against the dark silicate surface.

Erebo resides in a region of the system where the star Aurelius appears as little more than an exceptionally bright star. Consequently, the surface remains in a state of eternal twilight, with temperatures never rising high enough to allow even the most volatile gases to exist in anything but a frozen state.

==Discovery==
The discovery of Erebo was the culmination of a decades-long search for a trans-Centinelan object. Following the successful discovery of [[Centinela]] via orbital perturbations, astronomers theorized that an additional body might be responsible for residuals in the paths of the ice giants. On February 18, 1930 (Adonian Era), the Sinopian astronomer Aris Thorne (Ἄρης Θόρν) identified the planet using a blink comparator on photographic plates at the Kydonia Peak Observatory.

The discovery was initially controversial, as Erebo’s mass was found to be far too low to account for the perceived perturbations in [[Lucero]] or Centinela. Later analysis revealed that those residuals were the result of slightly inaccurate mass estimates for the ice giants themselves. Nevertheless, Thorne's discovery provided the first look into the true architecture of the outer Aurelius system.

==Physical characteristics==
===Surface geology===
The surface of Erebo is a landscape of frozen desolation. Analysis of the image provided by long-range probes reveals a primary crust of dark silicate rock and water ice, coated in a layer of tholins produced by billions of years of ultraviolet radiation reacting with methane. The resulting color is a somber, brownish-grey.

The white patches scattered across the surface are composed of nitrogen and methane ice. These "ice plains" are believed to be the result of a faint, seasonal atmosphere that sublimates when the planet reaches its perihelion (closest point to Aurelius) and freezes back onto the surface during its long trek into the dark. High-resolution imagery shows massive, ancient impact basins that have remained unchanged for eons, suggesting no geological activity for the majority of the planet's history.

===Atmosphere===
Erebo possesses a trace atmosphere that exists in a state of flux. For the majority of its 372-year orbit, the atmosphere is "collapsed," existing only as frost on the surface. As the planet approaches perihelion, a thin envelope of nitrogen and methane gas forms, creating a faint blue haze visible at the planet's limb. This atmosphere is extremely thin, with a surface pressure less than 1/100,000th that of [[Adonia]].

==Orbit and rotation==
Erebo follows a highly eccentric and inclined orbit, the most extreme of any major body in the Aurelius system. Its distance from the star varies significantly, ranging from 30 AU to 75 AU. At its closest point, it actually crosses the orbital path of [[Centinela]], though their specific orbital resonance prevents any risk of collision.

The planet rotates once every 153.3 hours, an unusually slow rotation likely caused by tidal interactions with its large, captured moon, Nyx. The two bodies are tidally locked to one another, meaning they always show the same face to each other as they orbit their common center of mass.

==Name and etymology==
The name Erebo is the Insulonian form of the Sinopian deity Erebus (Ἔρεβος), who represents the personification of primordial darkness. The name was proposed by Thorne to reflect the planet's location in the "eternal dark" of the outer system.

In Sanese astronomical nomenclature, the planet is known as Tokoyamisei (常闇星 [Tokoyamisei]), which translates to the Eternal Dark Star. This name emphasizes the absolute lack of warmth and light that defines the world, marking the final frontier before the void of the deep.

